    "top_frame": "Two walks, eight strikeouts, three runs. The math doesn't add up to losses.",
    "what_this_means": "The offense stalled when it mattered. Springs allowed only two earned runs across 5.1 innings, but the bats couldn't build on the moments they created. They left runners on base without consequence, scattered seven hits across nine innings, and never generated the kind of traffic that forces a Philadelphia pitcher to panic. Tyler Soderstrom's solo home run was the only clean strike. Nick Kurtz worked his at-bats. 2 for 4 with an RBI. But one RBI from two hits is not a recipe for winning road games. This is a team that's now lost two straight, and both games came when the pitching gave them reasonable chances to break through. The problem wasn't Springs. The problem was what followed.",
    "what_to_watch": "Division pressure doesn't wait for lineups to catch up. The Phillies sit in the same AL West frame, and every game in this stretch matters. At .248 as a team, .722 OPS, the offensive floor keeps getting lower. Losses like this one stack quietly. Not because they're unexpected, but because they're consistent. They had their moment. They didn't make it matter.",
